  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1030 PM MDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nIt is looking more and more likely that we have seen the last of\nthe triple digit heat for this year. As we move further into\nSeptember it gets harder and harder to hit 100F. Case and point,\nThursday's record high temperature is only 101F. Now we may be\nfinished with the triple digit heat, but that does not mean any \ngreat cool down. High temperatures will continue to run several \ndegrees above average into the start of next week. Which means \nlowland high temperatures will be in the mid to upper 90's through\nthe weekend. \n\nAnother factor helping to shave a few degrees of our high\ntemperatures will be the increase in moisture. Precipitable water\nvalues (PW's) will continue to run in the range of 1.2 to 1.3\nthrough next Tuesday. This will be enough moisture to help fuel\nscattered mountain and isolated lowland thunderstorms each day through\nthe weekend and into the start of next week. We won't see rain \nall over the place each afternoon, but we should see a little \nbetter rain coverage than we have seen the last few days. Looking \njust beyond the forecast period there are some significant \ndifferences in the rain chances. The GFS parks our old friend the \nupper level ridge over the southeast U.S. which allows copious \namounts of moisture and rain chances to move into the region from \nnorthern Mexico for the middle and end of next week. But the ECMWF\nkeeps a lobe of the upper level ridge leaning into New Mexico and\nthat really limits any moisture influx getting into the area. \nConsidering how our summer has been going, I'm tending to learn \nmore toward the drier solution.",
